    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWindow-local options are being applied inconsistently when ui2 is active vs. when not. When ui2 is not active, window-local options, e.g., spell, that are set in the first window for the first buffer, are copied over to subsequent windows, when that buffer is displayed. When ui2 is active, window-local options are not copied over to subsequent windows displaying the buffer. Instead, the global default values are applied. This is only the case for the first buffer that is being displayed, i.e., when running `nvim --clean -u minimal.lua minimal.lua`. When the buffer is opened only after the startup screen or the directory are already displayed, i.e., `:e minimal.lua`, both approaches behave the same.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nnvim --clean -u minimal.lua minimal.lua\n\n:set spell?\nnospell\n\n:setlocal spell\n\n:vnew\n\n:bprev\n\n:set spell?\nnospell\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nI am honestly not sure what the expected behavior would be. My reading of the documentation is that the window-local options from the first window should be applied to the second window as well: \"When editing a buffer that has been edited before, the options from the window that was last closed are used again.\" In any case, one would expect the behavior to be consistent irrespective of whether ui2 is active or not.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.6 Build type: Release LuaJIT 2.1.1785763465\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS 27.0.1\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nnixpkgs",
